1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,416
This video shows how to debug the Markets Intelligence repository on your own Mac

2
00:00:04,416 --> 00:00:08,722
with Docker Desktop and Docker Compose, and then how to deploy the backend, the

3
00:00:08,722 --> 00:00:13,302
frontend and the database to the cloud. Everything shown on screen was captured from

4
00:00:13,302 --> 00:00:15,428
the real stack running on this machine.

5
00:00:16,128 --> 00:00:20,784
In part one we start the Compose stack, check its health, read logs, trace one

6
00:00:20,784 --> 00:00:25,798
request by its id, look inside the containers and the database, and fix the failures

7
00:00:25,798 --> 00:00:30,812
you are most likely to meet. In part two we deploy the same images to the cloud with

8
00:00:30,812 --> 00:00:32,483
a managed Postgres database.

9
00:00:33,183 --> 00:00:38,373
Here is the stack. The web container is nginx on port 8080: it serves the built

10
00:00:38,373 --> 00:00:43,562
React app and forwards slash API and /health to the API container on port 8000.

11
00:00:43,562 --> 00:00:48,948
The API talks to Postgres through psycopg, and to Redis for caching and job locks.

12
00:00:48,948 --> 00:00:53,152
The migrate container applies the database migrations and exits.

13
00:00:53,152 --> 00:00:58,670
The worker runs the scheduled jobs. In live mode, market data comes from the NSE and

14
00:00:58,670 --> 00:01:02,808
Kite MCP servers; the mock MCP container is only for demo mode.

15
00:01:03,508 --> 00:01:07,735
Before you start, make sure Docker Desktop is running and not paused.

16
00:01:07,735 --> 00:01:12,697
A paused Docker cannot start containers, and it keeps old port mappings reserved.

17
00:01:12,697 --> 00:01:17,476
Create your .env file from .env.example, and keep comments on their own lines,

18
00:01:17,476 --> 00:01:21,825
because Compose passes a trailing comment through as part of the value.

19
00:01:21,825 --> 00:01:26,175
Choose demo mode for synthetic data, or live mode for the real servers.

20
00:01:26,875 --> 00:01:31,131
Start everything with docker compose up, -d, --build, --wait.

21
00:01:31,131 --> 00:01:36,642
Add the demo profile in demo mode. The wait flag returns only when every health

22
00:01:36,642 --> 00:01:41,386
check passes, or when a container fails. Then run docker compose ps.

23
00:01:41,386 --> 00:01:47,177
Here every long-running service is up and healthy, and the ports are published only

24
00:01:47,177 --> 00:01:50,805
on 127.0.0.1, so nothing is exposed to your network.

25
00:01:51,505 --> 00:01:54,589
Docker Desktop shows the same things graphically.

26
00:01:54,589 --> 00:01:58,554
Under Containers, open the hackathon group to see each service.

27
00:01:58,554 --> 00:02:03,526
For any container, the Logs tab streams its logs, Exec opens a shell inside it,

28
00:02:03,526 --> 00:02:08,498
Inspect shows its environment and health-check results, and Stats shows CPU and

29
00:02:08,498 --> 00:02:13,407
memory. Under Volumes, the pgdata volume holds the database files; deleting it

30
00:02:13,407 --> 00:02:14,477
deletes all data.

31
00:02:15,177 --> 00:02:18,039
When something looks wrong, check health first.

32
00:02:18,039 --> 00:02:22,485
Slash health slash ready through nginx on port 8080 tests the whole path.

33
00:02:22,485 --> 00:02:27,418
Database and cache must be ok. The market data sources are reported here too, but

34
00:02:27,418 --> 00:02:32,533
they never make the API unready, because stored data can still be served when NSE is

35
00:02:32,533 --> 00:02:32,838
down.

36
00:02:33,538 --> 00:02:38,462
Next, open the Data status page. It shows each connector's state, the tools it

37
00:02:38,462 --> 00:02:41,746
advertises, latency, error rate, and its last error.

38
00:02:41,746 --> 00:02:46,544
In this real capture, the NSE bhavcopy server had a 30% error rate from read

39
00:02:46,544 --> 00:02:51,848
timeouts that day, and the Kite server shows seven allowed tools and fifteen blocked

40
00:02:51,848 --> 00:02:57,026
trading tools. Below it you find stored data counts, the broker login, and the job

41
00:02:57,026 --> 00:02:57,531
history.

42
00:02:58,231 --> 00:03:02,662
Logs are the next stop. Follow the API with docker compose logs -f API.

43
00:03:02,662 --> 00:03:07,904
The worker logs every scheduled job, and the migrate container tells you whether the

44
00:03:07,904 --> 00:03:12,959
schema was applied. Each request is one JSON line with its method, route, status,

45
00:03:12,959 --> 00:03:14,582
duration and a request id.

46
00:03:15,282 --> 00:03:20,229
Every error the app returns carries a request id, and the web app shows it next to

47
00:03:20,229 --> 00:03:23,547
the message. Copy that id and grep the API logs for it.

48
00:03:23,547 --> 00:03:28,614
Here a lookup of a symbol that does not exist returned a 404 with request id 477af…,

49
00:03:28,614 --> 00:03:31,811
and the matching log line shows the route and timing.

50
00:03:32,511 --> 00:03:37,174
To see what a container really received, run the pre-flight checks inside it.

51
00:03:37,174 --> 00:03:41,715
Show prints the effective settings with the password hidden, db connects to

52
00:03:41,715 --> 00:03:46,681
Postgres, and Redis pings the cache. Inside Compose, the API uses the postgres and

53
00:03:46,681 --> 00:03:51,041
redis service names. You can also open a shell, or list the environment.

54
00:03:51,741 --> 00:03:57,041
For data questions, open SQL inside the postgres container with docker compose exec

55
00:03:57,041 --> 00:04:02,405
postgres psql. The job runs table shows every refresh job with its status and error,

56
00:04:02,405 --> 00:04:06,301
and alembic version shows which migration the database is at.

57
00:04:06,301 --> 00:04:10,005
From your Mac, any database tool can connect to port 5432.

58
00:04:10,705 --> 00:04:14,135
These are the failures we actually hit on this machine.

59
00:04:14,135 --> 00:04:19,311
If port 8000 is taken, an earlier Compose stack is holding it, even while Docker is

60
00:04:19,311 --> 00:04:24,114
paused; stop it with docker compose down. If the migrate container fails with

61
00:04:24,114 --> 00:04:29,165
password authentication failed, the database volume still has the password it was

62
00:04:29,165 --> 00:04:33,032
created with, because Postgres applies the password only once.

63
00:04:33,032 --> 00:04:36,338
Put the original password back, or change it in psql.

64
00:04:37,038 --> 00:04:41,795
The API refuses to start on a database without the current schema, and tells you to

65
00:04:41,795 --> 00:04:46,208
run preflight migrate. That command also repairs a database whose tables were

66
00:04:46,208 --> 00:04:50,679
created without a migration record. NSE outages appear as unavailable with the

67
00:04:50,679 --> 00:04:55,436
reason, never as invented data. And the Kite broker needs your approval through the

68
00:04:55,436 --> 00:04:57,098
link on the Data status page.

69
00:04:57,798 --> 00:05:03,214
After a code change, rebuild only what changed: API and worker for backend code, web

70
00:05:03,214 --> 00:05:07,985
for frontend code. Run the migrate service again after adding a migration.

71
00:05:07,985 --> 00:05:11,402
The database volume is kept, so you do not lose data.

72
00:05:12,102 --> 00:05:17,195
For step-through debugging, keep Postgres and Redis in Docker and run the API on

73
00:05:17,195 --> 00:05:22,353
your Mac. Stop the API, worker and web containers, then run scripts/dev.sh, which

74
00:05:22,353 --> 00:05:27,637
finds the Compose database, applies migrations, and starts the API and the web app.

75
00:05:27,637 --> 00:05:32,858
Or launch the API under your editor's debugger with a configuration like this one,

76
00:05:32,858 --> 00:05:35,660
and set breakpoints anywhere in the backend.

77
00:05:36,360 --> 00:05:41,274
Stop keeps everything. Down removes the containers but keeps the database volume.

78
00:05:41,274 --> 00:05:46,067
Down dash V also deletes the volume and all data, so take a backup with pg_dump

79
00:05:46,067 --> 00:05:47,706
first if you might need it.

80
00:05:48,406 --> 00:05:53,806
Part two. We deploy the same two images, the API and the web app, next to a managed

81
00:05:53,806 --> 00:05:59,207
Postgres database. The complete commands for Azure, AWS and Google Cloud are in the

82
00:05:59,207 --> 00:06:02,070
RUN_AND_DEPLOY guide in the analysis folder.

83
00:06:02,770 --> 00:06:08,469
The recommended first layout is one unit with two containers side by side: nginx on

84
00:06:08,469 --> 00:06:14,168
port 8080 takes the public traffic and forwards API calls over 127.0.0.1 to the API

85
00:06:14,168 --> 00:06:19,661
on port 8000. One replica runs the scheduler itself, so Redis is not needed yet.

86
00:06:19,661 --> 00:06:25,223
The database is a managed Postgres, a migration job runs before each new version,

87
00:06:25,223 --> 00:06:28,313
and secrets live in the cloud's secret store.

88
00:06:29,013 --> 00:06:34,358
Step one. Build both images for linux amd64 and push them to your cloud's container

89
00:06:34,358 --> 00:06:39,703
registry. This matters on an Apple Silicon Mac, which builds ARM images by default,

90
00:06:39,703 --> 00:06:43,052
and most cloud container services will refuse those.

91
00:06:43,752 --> 00:06:47,382
Step two. Create a managed Postgres sixteen database.

92
00:06:47,382 --> 00:06:52,929
This example is Google Cloud SQL; Azure Database for Postgres and Amazon RDS work

93
00:06:52,929 --> 00:06:58,545
the same way. The connection string uses the psycopg driver, with SSL mode require

94
00:06:58,545 --> 00:07:03,201
for a network connection, or the Cloud SQL socket path on Cloud Run.

95
00:07:03,901 --> 00:07:09,786
Step three. Put the database URL, an API token and any model token in the secret

96
00:07:09,786 --> 00:07:13,832
store. Set app env to production and data mode to live.

97
00:07:13,832 --> 00:07:19,275
Leave auto create schema false, because the migration job owns the schema.

98
00:07:19,275 --> 00:07:24,866
And on the web container, set API upstream to 127.0.0.1 colon 8000, so nginx

99
00:07:24,866 --> 00:07:30,236
forwards to the API next to it. In Compose it defaults to API colon 8000.

100
00:07:30,936 --> 00:07:35,978
Step four. Run the migration once, as a one-off job with the API image and the

101
00:07:35,978 --> 00:07:41,344
command alembic upgrade head. On Cloud Run that is a job, on Azure a Container Apps

102
00:07:41,344 --> 00:07:45,869
job, on AWS a one-off ECS task. Run it again before every new version.

103
00:07:46,569 --> 00:07:52,212
Step five. Deploy the two containers together. On Cloud Run, one command defines the

104
00:07:52,212 --> 00:07:57,048
web container as the public entry on port 8080 and the API as a sidecar.

105
00:07:57,048 --> 00:08:02,422
Exactly one instance, and no CPU throttling, so the twelve hour and monthly jobs

106
00:08:02,422 --> 00:08:07,460
keep running. Azure uses a two container app defined in YAML, and AWS a two

107
00:08:07,460 --> 00:08:10,012
container task behind a load balancer.

108
00:08:10,712 --> 00:08:16,043
Step six. Check /health/ready on the public URL, then set CORS_ORIGINS to that URL.

109
00:08:16,043 --> 00:08:21,373
Open the app, enter the API token under Access on the Data status page, approve the

110
00:08:21,373 --> 00:08:26,768
Kite broker session, and run refresh all data. Follow the logs with your cloud's log

111
00:08:26,768 --> 00:08:29,465
tail command while the first refresh runs.

112
00:08:30,165 --> 00:08:35,089
To ship a new version: push images with a new tag, run the migration job with the

113
00:08:35,089 --> 00:08:39,829
new image, then deploy the new tags. To grow beyond one replica, add a managed

114
00:08:39,829 --> 00:08:44,813
Redis, turn the scheduler off on the web and API unit, raise its replicas, and run

115
00:08:44,813 --> 00:08:47,487
one always-on worker that runs the schedule.

116
00:08:48,187 --> 00:08:52,893
To be clear about what was tested: the Docker Compose stack and the web container's

117
00:08:52,893 --> 00:08:57,655
API address were verified on this machine. The cloud commands follow each platform's

118
00:08:57,655 --> 00:09:02,418
standard approach but were not run against a real cloud account, so treat your first

119
00:09:02,418 --> 00:09:06,783
deployment as a trial. The how to run guide, the RUN_AND_DEPLOY guide and the

120
00:09:06,783 --> 00:09:10,355
architecture document in the analysis folder have every detail.

121
00:09:10,355 --> 00:09:11,489
Thanks for watching.
